  "content": "\\section{Related Work}\n\nThis section presents a comprehensive review of existing research related to Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol with Real-Time Traffic Information for Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) Networks. We examine four key research domains: CPI routing optimization in logistics, carbon-aware routing protocols, real-time traffic integration in routing, and Cyber-Physical Internet frameworks for logistics.\n\n\\subsection{CPI and Physical Internet in Logistics}\n\nThe concept of Physical Internet (PI) emerged as a solution to address the challenges of global unsustainability in logistics networks. First proposed conceptually in The Economist \\cite{economist2006pi} and later formalized by Montreuil \\cite{montreuil2011toward}, the PI aims to establish an open global logistics system based on physical, digital, and operational interconnection through encapsulation, interfaces, and protocols. The fundamental principle underlying the PI is similar to that of the digital internet - creating a standardized, open network for moving physical goods efficiently.\n\nAs logistics networks have become increasingly complex and fragmented, the PI offers a promising approach to achieve greater efficiency and sustainability. Sarraj et al. \\cite{sarraj2014analogies} highlighted the potential advantages of interconnected logistics networks over fragmented ones, demonstrating that networks in the PI can benefit from shared resources such as containers, load transfers, transfer hubs, and logistics providers.\n\nBuilding upon the PI concept, the Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) has emerged as a new paradigm that strengthens the synergistic use of cyber capabilities to improve the operation and execution of PI networks \\cite{wu2024cyber}. Wu et al. \\cite{wu2024cyber} defined CPI as an extension of PI with an additional cyber layer that enhances information synchronization and decision-making. In CPI networks, the locations and states of containers in the physical world are collected, communicated, and synchronized in real-time, while container flows are configured, supervised, and manipulated through information flows in cyberspace.\n\nRecent research has explored various applications of CPI in logistics. Qu et al. \\cite{qu2024routing} proposed a framework of CPI networks for B2B e-commerce logistics, introducing components such as CPI routers, routing tables, and autonomous logistics systems (ALSs) to integrate fragmented logistics networks. Their approach demonstrated how the concept of datagram routing in digital networks could be applied to physical package routing in CPI networks for more economical and efficient logistics operations.\n\n\\subsection{Carbon-Aware Routing Protocols}\n\nWith growing environmental concerns, carbon-aware routing protocols have gained significant attention in logistics and transportation research. These protocols aim to optimize transportation routes to minimize carbon emissions while maintaining operational efficiency.\n\nNg et al. \\cite{ng2024carbon} developed a carbon-aware routing protocol specifically for modular construction logistics. Their approach integrates a unique set of criteria, including module weight, travel distance, and carbon emission factors, within a life cycle assessment framework to provide routing decisions that minimize carbon emissions. Their protocol operates within a CPI network framework designed to facilitate integrated solutions for multiple actors in modular construction logistics.\n\nThe carbon-aware routing protocol proposed by Ng et al. \\cite{ng2024carbon} considers emissions as the primary metric in the routing table, departing from traditional routing approaches that typically optimize for distance, time, or cost. Their numerical studies demonstrated significant reductions in carbon emissions compared to conventional routing methods, highlighting the potential of carbon-aware routing protocols to contribute to sustainability goals in the construction industry.\n\nHowever, existing carbon-aware routing protocols primarily focus on static optimization based on predefined parameters and often fail to adapt to changing traffic conditions in real-time. As noted by Peng et al. \\cite{peng2020promoting}, while current protocols can optimize for carbon emissions, they typically do not incorporate dynamic traffic data that could significantly impact the actual emissions produced during transportation due to varying speeds, congestion, and detours.\n\n\\subsection{Real-Time Traffic Integration in Routing}\n\nIntegrating real-time traffic information into routing decisions has been a focus of research in intelligent transportation systems. Traditional approaches to routing in logistics networks often rely on static parameters, which can lead to suboptimal routes when traffic conditions change unexpectedly.\n\nRecent advances in IoT, sensor networks, and data analytics have enabled more sophisticated approaches to traffic-aware routing. Zhang et al. \\cite{zhang2017optimal} proposed a collaborative transportation service trading system for B2B e-commerce logistics that incorporates real-time traffic data to optimize routing decisions. Their approach demonstrated improvements in delivery time and resource utilization by dynamically adjusting routes based on current traffic conditions.\n\nIn the context of cyber-physical systems, real-time data collection and analysis have become increasingly important for adaptive routing. Zhao et al. \\cite{zhao2021cyber} developed a cyber-physical spatial-temporal analytics framework for smart contact tracing that demonstrated the potential of real-time data integration for dynamic decision-making. Similar principles can be applied to logistics routing to enable adaptive responses to changing traffic conditions.\n\nKong et al. \\cite{kong2021cyber} explored the use of cyber-physical systems for on-demand logistics trading, highlighting how real-time information can facilitate more efficient resource allocation and routing in dynamic environments. Their research emphasized the importance of timely information flow between physical and cyber components of logistics systems.\n\nHowever, existing approaches to traffic-adaptive routing typically focus on time or cost optimization without considering environmental impacts such as carbon emissions. This creates a gap in the literature where real-time traffic adaptation and carbon awareness remain largely disconnected in current routing protocols.\n\n\\subsection{Cyber-Physical Internet Frameworks for Logistics}\n\nThe development of comprehensive frameworks for CPI in logistics has been an active area of research in recent years. These frameworks aim to provide structured approaches to implementing CPI concepts in practical logistics operations.\n\nWu et al. \\cite{wu2024cyber} proposed a CPI-LI2 (logistics infrastructure integration) framework with a two-layer structure to enable logistics infrastructure integration in the Greater Bay Area of China. Their framework includes a link layer responsible for dividing autonomous areas and electing key autonomous areas and regional link centers, and a transport layer responsible for making routing decisions based on real-time data. The authors demonstrated the effectiveness of their framework through a case study of medical logistics in the Greater Bay Area, showing its adaptability to large-scale logistics networks with dynamically changing links.\n\nQu et al. \\cite{qu2024routing} developed a framework for CPI networks in B2B e-commerce logistics that integrates logistics nodes into CPI routers with both internet connections in the cyber layer and physical connections for transporting packages. Their framework includes routing protocols within and among autonomous logistics systems (ALSs) to make efficient and economical routing decisions based on information in routing tables.\n\nAddressing the operational aspects of CPI networks, Lee et al. \\cite{lee2015cyber} proposed a 5C framework for cyber-physical systems that emphasizes the key role of digital interoperability in establishing interconnection between cyber and physical layers. Building on this foundation, Zhang et al. \\cite{zhang2018smart} developed a smart logistics path decision system with physical, cloud network, and application layers, incorporating intelligent algorithms for path optimization.\n\nWhile these frameworks have made significant contributions to the understanding and implementation of CPI in logistics, there remains a need for frameworks that specifically address the integration of carbon awareness with real-time traffic information in routing decisions.\n\n\\subsection{Research Gaps and Our Contribution}\n\nOur review of the literature reveals several important gaps that our research aims to address:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Integration gap:} Existing carbon-aware routing protocols typically do not incorporate real-time traffic information, leading to potentially suboptimal routes when traffic conditions change unexpectedly. Conversely, traffic-adaptive routing approaches generally do not consider carbon emissions as a key optimization parameter.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Framework gap:} While various CPI frameworks have been proposed for logistics, there is a lack of comprehensive frameworks that specifically integrate carbon awareness with real-time traffic adaptation in routing decisions.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Implementation gap:} Practical implementations of carbon-aware adaptive routing protocols in CPI networks remain limited, with few studies demonstrating their effectiveness in realistic logistics scenarios.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nOur research addresses these gaps by proposing a Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol with Real-Time Traffic Information for CPI Networks. This protocol integrates both carbon emission optimization and real-time traffic adaptation using a two-layer framework, enabling more efficient, sustainable, and responsive logistics operations. Our approach brings together the environmental benefits of carbon-aware routing with the operational advantages of real-time traffic adaptation, providing a comprehensive solution to the challenges faced in modern logistics networks.\n",
